{"type":"standard","title":"Contractible space","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Contractible space</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q230689","titles":{"canonical":"Contractible_space","normalized":"Contractible space","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Contractible space</span></span>"},"pageid":1752414,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Contractibility_figure.png/330px-Contractibility_figure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":330,"height":246},"originalimage":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/Contractibility_figure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","width":682,"height":509},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1346982999","tid":"cb3887e0-2fbd-11f1-9f03-1e8b5bd318d6","timestamp":"2026-04-04T00:32:43Z","description":"Can be continuously shrunk to a point","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contractible_space","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contractible_space?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contractible_space?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Contractible_space"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contractible_space","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Contractible_space","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contractible_space?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Contractible_space"}},"extract":"In mathematics, a topological space X is contractible if the identity map on X is null-homotopic, i.e. if it is homotopic to some constant map. Intuitively, a contractible space is one that can be continuously shrunk to a point within that space.","extract_html":"<p>In mathematics, a topological space <i>X</i> is <b>contractible</b> if the identity map on <i>X</i> is null-homotopic, i.e. if it is homotopic to some constant map. Intuitively, a contractible space is one that can be continuously shrunk to a point within that space.</p>"}